
1. Create ECR Repository

AWS Console → ECR → Repositories → Create repository

We used:

Plain Text
Repository type: Private
Repository name: harish-ecs-demo

Create the repository.

Purpose

ECR = Elastic Container Registry

It stores Docker/container images.

We didn't actually use our ECR image in today's Nginx deployment. We created the repository so we can use it in the next exercise.
------------------------------------------------
2. Create ECS Cluster

AWS Console → ECS → Clusters → Create cluster

Cluster name:

Plain Text
harish-ecs-cluster

Use AWS Fargate/serverless infrastructure.

No EC2 instance is required.

Purpose

The ECS cluster is the logical environment where ECS workloads are managed.
----------------------------------------------------------
3. Create Task Definition

Go to:

ECS → Task definitions → Create new task definition

Task definition family:

Plain Text
harish-nginx-task

Infrastructure:

Plain Text
AWS Fargate

For a small practice workload:

Plain Text
CPU: 0.25 vCPU
Memory: 0.5 GB
Container configuration

Container name:

Plain Text
nginx

Image:

Plain Text
nginx:latest

Container port:

Plain Text
80

Protocol:

Plain Text
TCP

Create the Task Definition.

Purpose

Think of a Task Definition as a blueprint.

It tells ECS things such as:

Plain Text
Which image?
Which port?
How much CPU?
How much memory?
What logging?
What IAM roles?
----------------------------------------------------
4. Create ECS Service

Open:

ECS → Clusters → harish-ecs-cluster

Open the dropdown next to Create with Express Mode.

Choose:

Plain Text
Create service

We did not choose:

Plain Text
Run task
Create daemon
Create scheduled task
====================================================
5. Configure Fargate

Configure the service approximately as:

Plain Text
Compute option: Launch type
Launch type: FARGATE
Platform version: LATEST

Task definition:

Plain Text
harish-nginx-task

Revision:

Plain Text
Latest

Desired tasks:

Plain Text
1

This means:

ECS should maintain one running copy of the container task.
========================================================================

6. Configure Networking

We selected the VPC/subnets and enabled:

Plain Text
Auto-assign public IP: ON

The Fargate task therefore received its own ENI/network interface and public IP.

The task used network mode:

Plain Text
awsvpc

No Load Balancer was configured for this first exercise.
=================================================================

7. Configure Security Group

This was an important troubleshooting step.

The Security Group must permit the Nginx traffic:

Plain Text
Type: HTTP
Protocol: TCP
Port: 80
Source: My IP

For a temporary lab test, we can alternatively use:

Plain Text
Type: HTTP
Protocol: TCP
Port: 80
Source: 0.0.0.0/0

For regular use, restrict the source rather than leaving unnecessary public access.
============================================================================================

8. Create the Service

After creating the service, go to:

ECS → Cluster → Tasks

We confirmed:

Plain Text
Task count: 1/1
Last status: Running
Desired status: Running
Task definition: harish-nginx-task:1

This confirmed that Fargate successfully started our Nginx container.
==================================================================================

9. Find Fargate Public IP

Go to:

Cluster → Tasks → click Task ID → Networking

We found:

Plain Text
Network mode: awsvpc
Private IP: 172.31.28.251
Public IP: 3.26.180.123
 

Note: Public IP addresses can change after task replacement/redeployment, so don't treat the IP above as permanent.
==========================================================================================

10. Test the Application

Open:

Plain Text
http://<FARGATE-PUBLIC-IP>

Example from our practice:

Plain Text
http://3.26.180.123

We initially couldn't access the page.

We checked the Security Group / port 80 access, corrected the network access, and then Nginx became accessible. ✅

Expected page:

Plain Text
Welcome to nginx!
